[[:db/retract 17592186188931 :question/answer-fields 17592186187632] {:db/id 17592186188279 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-838322c6-867e-5a73-a18d-bbf81bd1218b" "question-b72abcb6-85da-5c4d-a8eb-72ce20ae6b72"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188931 :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,3x+2y-z=0}$ then $\\text{nullity}(T)=1$\n- If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T}$ then $T$ is invertible\n- If ${v}_{1},$ ${v}_{2},$ and ${v}_{3}$ are the columns of $T$ and ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2}$, then $\\text{Ker}(T)={0}$" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Solving $3x+2y-z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=3x+2y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ 3x+2y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. If ${v}_{1}$ and ${v}_{2}$ are two different solutions to $Tx=[1,-2,1]^{T},$ then \n\n$$\nT{v}_{1}=T{v}_{2}\\,⟹\\,T({v}_{1}-{v}_{2})=0,\n$$\n\nwhere ${v}_{1}-{v}_{2}$ is a non-zero vector. This means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Null}(T)\\ne {0}$ and, according to the invertible matrix theorem, $T$ can't be invertible.\n\n- Statement III is false. If the columns of $T$ are such that ${v}_{3}={v}_{1}+2{v}_{2},$ then the set of vectors ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3}}$ is linearly dependent and therefore $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Col}(T)\\ne {R}^{3}.$ Now, according to the matrix invertible theorem, we obtain that $\\text{Ker}(T)\\ne {0}.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1a41ad32-7f22-5720-8aeb-d6772bd219f3"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1a41ad32-7f22-5720-8aeb-d6772bd219f3" :answer-field/id #uuid "1a41ad32-7f22-5720-8aeb-d6772bd219f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-82cdbbed-d3b0-5cbd-98ec-7fd44334fdc9" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8c7f5f0b-b8d0-5234-8de6-49e97dadce22" "answer-6da2df3c-89eb-5372-97ad-f660691bfcb8" "answer-82cdbbed-d3b0-5cbd-98ec-7fd44334fdc9" "answer-d681f156-99f3-5753-a570-2bb3e53d88eb" "answer-5540b73e-76fe-5b3b-9e59-bcdd8e64db55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8c7f5f0b-b8d0-5234-8de6-49e97dadce22" :answer/id #uuid "8c7f5f0b-b8d0-5234-8de6-49e97dadce22" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6da2df3c-89eb-5372-97ad-f660691bfcb8" :answer/id #uuid "6da2df3c-89eb-5372-97ad-f660691bfcb8" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82cdbbed-d3b0-5cbd-98ec-7fd44334fdc9" :answer/id #uuid "82cdbbed-d3b0-5cbd-98ec-7fd44334fdc9" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d681f156-99f3-5753-a570-2bb3e53d88eb" :answer/id #uuid "d681f156-99f3-5753-a570-2bb3e53d88eb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5540b73e-76fe-5b3b-9e59-bcdd8e64db55" :answer/id #uuid "5540b73e-76fe-5b3b-9e59-bcdd8e64db55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186188287 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-94b1ad7b-577b-5c7c-8164-0f25bc8b5263" "question-7bb2599b-79c3-5637-9b4b-ca26bce210be"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id "question-94b1ad7b-577b-5c7c-8164-0f25bc8b5263" :question/id #uuid "94b1ad7b-577b-5c7c-8164-0f25bc8b5263" :question/math-academy-id "q-83446" :question/problem "Find $\\text{rank}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,x+y+z=0}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $x+y+z=0$ for $z,$ we get $z=-x-y.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ -x-y\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+y{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-6c728edd-0030-5f84-bc57-b18190d3fb0e"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-6c728edd-0030-5f84-bc57-b18190d3fb0e" :answer-field/id #uuid "6c728edd-0030-5f84-bc57-b18190d3fb0e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer-field/choices ["answer-af65c1f2-8ae8-5564-a484-7a9735e719d2" "answer-e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" "answer-c038e33b-b13d-5ebb-aa6f-234d43c1d51b" "answer-a80e2855-a004-5120-bf2a-4803a51aedf5" "answer-1470852b-03e9-5db4-8d33-ce40b9b312ff"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-af65c1f2-8ae8-5564-a484-7a9735e719d2" :answer/id #uuid "af65c1f2-8ae8-5564-a484-7a9735e719d2" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer/id #uuid "e9530934-50fc-57c8-a93e-515a75115d2d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c038e33b-b13d-5ebb-aa6f-234d43c1d51b" :answer/id #uuid "c038e33b-b13d-5ebb-aa6f-234d43c1d51b" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a80e2855-a004-5120-bf2a-4803a51aedf5" :answer/id #uuid "a80e2855-a004-5120-bf2a-4803a51aedf5" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1470852b-03e9-5db4-8d33-ce40b9b312ff" :answer/id #uuid "1470852b-03e9-5db4-8d33-ce40b9b312ff" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-838322c6-867e-5a73-a18d-bbf81bd1218b" :question/id #uuid "838322c6-867e-5a73-a18d-bbf81bd1218b" :question/math-academy-id "q-84081" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T.$ Which of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $\\text{Ker}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y=z=0}$ then $\\text{rank}(T)=2$\n- If $Tx=[1,0,0]^{T}$ has more than one solution, then $\\text{nullity}(T)=0$\n- If $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}$, then $T$ is invertible" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{3}$ into ${R}^{3},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{3} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=3 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=3.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Since $y=z=0,$ any vector from $\\text{Ker}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ y \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ Finally, according the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{rank}(T) & =3-\\text{nullity}(T) \\\\  & =3-1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Since $Tx=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$ has more than one solution, there are ${u}_{1},{u}_{2}\\in {R}^{3}$ such that  ${u}_{1}\\ne {u}_{2}$ and \n\n$$\nT({u}_{1})=T({u}_{2})=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nThis means, $T$ is not a bijection and according to the invertible matrix theorem, $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0.$\n\n- Statement III is false. Notice that $\\text{Ker}(T)=\\text{Span}{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}.$ Therefore, $\\text{nullity}(T)=1.$ So, $T$ is not a bijection or, which is the same, $T$ is not invertible.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b752c3d9-0b1a-5d80-8a37-82a98660d651"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b752c3d9-0b1a-5d80-8a37-82a98660d651" :answer-field/id #uuid "b752c3d9-0b1a-5d80-8a37-82a98660d651" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f9f2e498-b357-5b66-a290-e994e329a0aa" :answer-field/choices ["answer-f2d76c7a-6c06-5532-924f-6597deab979a" "answer-f9f2e498-b357-5b66-a290-e994e329a0aa" "answer-398883aa-641c-5083-bd16-075d6b81fc9c" "answer-137c378d-550c-59a7-83a4-77963d65438e" "answer-f008c6f6-1933-5ee3-808d-05aba679cfa0"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f2d76c7a-6c06-5532-924f-6597deab979a" :answer/id #uuid "f2d76c7a-6c06-5532-924f-6597deab979a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f9f2e498-b357-5b66-a290-e994e329a0aa" :answer/id #uuid "f9f2e498-b357-5b66-a290-e994e329a0aa" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-398883aa-641c-5083-bd16-075d6b81fc9c" :answer/id #uuid "398883aa-641c-5083-bd16-075d6b81fc9c" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-137c378d-550c-59a7-83a4-77963d65438e" :answer/id #uuid "137c378d-550c-59a7-83a4-77963d65438e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f008c6f6-1933-5ee3-808d-05aba679cfa0" :answer/id #uuid "f008c6f6-1933-5ee3-808d-05aba679cfa0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-7bb2599b-79c3-5637-9b4b-ca26bce210be" :question/id #uuid "7bb2599b-79c3-5637-9b4b-ca26bce210be" :question/math-academy-id "q-140098" :question/problem "Find $\\text{nullity}(T)$ given that $T:{R}^{3}\\to {R}^{3}$ and $\\text{Im}(T)={[x,y,z]^{T}\\,:\\,y-2z=0}.$" :question/worked-solution "Solving $y-2z=0$ for $y,$ we get $y=2z.$ Therefore, any vector from $\\text{Im}(T)$ can be written as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[\\begin{aligned}x \\\\ 2z \\\\ z\\end{aligned}]=x{{[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{1}}+z{{[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]}_{⏟}}_{{v}_{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis means that $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2}}.$ Therefore, $\\text{rank}(T)=2.$ \n\nFinally, according to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =3-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =3-2 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-28b8502d-f01b-56da-9de3-fce2aa5353e1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-28b8502d-f01b-56da-9de3-fce2aa5353e1" :answer-field/id #uuid "28b8502d-f01b-56da-9de3-fce2aa5353e1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e84109bf-1001-5843-8805-1788b156098c" :answer-field/choices ["answer-cc277bfe-8316-5960-ad00-41d18d0bb79e" "answer-e84109bf-1001-5843-8805-1788b156098c" "answer-b15a9656-7038-5a55-bf9f-c3d46f455338" "answer-e7eb8255-65e0-55e8-9452-62dfb2f06a3d" "answer-0f3fa2ee-eac1-54f7-a4c6-ec5b539510f4"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cc277bfe-8316-5960-ad00-41d18d0bb79e" :answer/id #uuid "cc277bfe-8316-5960-ad00-41d18d0bb79e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "0" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e84109bf-1001-5843-8805-1788b156098c" :answer/id #uuid "e84109bf-1001-5843-8805-1788b156098c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b15a9656-7038-5a55-bf9f-c3d46f455338" :answer/id #uuid "b15a9656-7038-5a55-bf9f-c3d46f455338" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e7eb8255-65e0-55e8-9452-62dfb2f06a3d" :answer/id #uuid "e7eb8255-65e0-55e8-9452-62dfb2f06a3d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0f3fa2ee-eac1-54f7-a4c6-ec5b539510f4" :answer/id #uuid "0f3fa2ee-eac1-54f7-a4c6-ec5b539510f4" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "Not enough information." :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-b72abcb6-85da-5c4d-a8eb-72ce20ae6b72" :question/id #uuid "b72abcb6-85da-5c4d-a8eb-72ce20ae6b72" :question/math-academy-id "q-140100" :question/problem "Let $T:{R}^{5}\\to {R}^{5}$ be a linear transformation whose standard matrix is $T,$ and let ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ be a set of linearly independent vectors in ${R}^{5}.$ Consider the following statement:\n\n*If  $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$, then $\\text{__________}.$ * \n\nWhich of the following can be placed into the blank space to make a true statement?\n\n- $\\text{rank}(T)=4$\n- $\\text{nullity}(T)=4$\n- $T$ is a bijection" :question/worked-solution "Since $T$ is a linear transformation from ${R}^{5}$ into ${R}^{5},$ the invertible matrix theorem states the following:\n\n $T$ is a bijection (an invertible transformation) $\\,⟺\\,{\\,\\begin{aligned} & \\text{Im}(T)={R}^{5} \\\\  & \\text{rank}(T)=5 \\\\  & \\text{Ker}(T)={0} \\\\  & \\text{nullity}(T)=0\\end{aligned}$ \n\nAlso, according to the rank-nullity theorem, \n\n$$\n\\text{rank}(T)+\\text{nullity}(T)=5.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Indeed, since $\\text{Im}(T)=\\text{Span}{{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ and the set ${{v}_{1},{v}_{2},{v}_{3},{v}_{4}}$ is linearly independent, we get that $\\text{rank}(T)=\\text{rank}(T)=4.$\n\n- Statement II is false. According to the rank-nullity theorem, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{nullity}(T) & =5-\\text{rank}(T) \\\\  & =5-4 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since $\\text{rank}(T)\\ne 5$ (or since $\\text{nullity}(T)\\ne 0$), the linear transformation $T$ cannot be a bijection according to the invertible matrix theorem.\n\nTherefore, the correct option is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-0afa56a8-491c-5f58-8d6a-30c34a607288"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-0afa56a8-491c-5f58-8d6a-30c34a607288" :answer-field/id #uuid "0afa56a8-491c-5f58-8d6a-30c34a607288" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-2c9cc9f1-6dc9-5ef5-bc2e-1c6223fff913" :answer-field/choices ["answer-6c1ecd08-5cfd-5f